<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-automation-tool-roadmap" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Automation Tool Roadmap</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Purpose</term>. The aim of this tool is to support the analysis of open-ended student responses using 
      Balacheff's conception framework, with particular attention to the control structures students appear to use 
      when justifying mathematical claims.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Background</term>. A <term>control structure</term>, in the sense used here, is the criterion or rule a 
      student appears to use to recognize that they have reached a settled answer to a problem 
      <xref ref="bib-balacheff-gaudin-2010"/>. In the linear-algebra question set this tool targets, control structures
      include criteria such as <q>there is a free variable</q>, <q>the number of pivots equals the number of rows</q>, 
      and <q>the system is consistent</q>. The codebook used by the group names ten such control structures 
      (<c>CS1</c>&#x2013;<c>CS10</c>). Surfacing <em>which</em> control structure a student appears to be using, and 
      whether more than one is in play, is the central interpretive task this tool is intended to support.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Motivation</term>. The tool should provide one or more of the following benefits: 
      <ul marker="square">
        <li>
          Make the coding process more consistent, transparent, and scalable while preserving the interpretive role 
          of human researchers.
        </li>
        <li>
          Help organize student data, identify codable reasoning, surface possible control structures, and flag 
          ambiguities/patterns in the coding or data.
        </li>
        <li>
          Provide a platform for researchers to visualize, audit, and calibrate the coding process.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Design</term>. This report describes the <term>Short Answer</term> service, the question type the tool was
      first built around. The tool as a whole (PROTEUS) is now organized as a service-agnostic core&#x2014;shared ingest
      and storage&#x2014;with one service per question type; the short-answer service is the one organized as the five
      small stages described here, and other question types (such as drag-and-drop) are handled by their own services
      over the same core. Each stage reads a simple table and writes a slightly richer one. To make the design concrete,
      the same four sample responses are traced from beginning to end below, so the effect of each stage on the data is
      visible at a glance. The new columns that each stage adds are called out under every output table, since those are
      the columns the research team will spend the most time looking at.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-cast">
      <title>The four sample responses traced in this report.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="8%"/>
        <col width="49%" />
        <col width="43%" />
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium" halign="center">
          <cell><p>Tag</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What the student wrote</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What it illustrates</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
          <cell><p>There is a free variable so it is not possible to have a unique solution.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A clean, single-criterion justification.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
          <cell><p><em>(blank)</em></p></cell>
          <cell><p>An uncodable response that should be excluded.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              One and only one solution is not possible because there will be a free variable since there are more 
              variables than rows.
            </p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>A response that combines different criteria and may match multiple control structures.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
          <cell><p>It depends what is in the augmented matrix.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A response with reasoning outside the current codebook.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-stage-1">
    <title>Stage 1: Ingestor</title>

    <p>
      The <q>ingestor</q> takes student responses from Runestone data files and converts them into a consistent table. 
      This stage does not interpret the response or assign any codes. It only prepares the data for analysis.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Stage 1: Ingestor - Input &amp; Output</title>
      <sidebyside valign="middle" widths="15% 5% 15% 5% 25%" margins="17% 17%">
        <p>Raw export rows</p>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-1-diagram">
          <mermaid>
            flowchart
              A(Ingestion)
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-1-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              RESPONSE
              RESPONSE {
                response_id *
                student_id *
                question_id *
                response_text *
                ⋮ *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Input: DataShop (TAB, <delete>CSV</delete>)</title>

      <p>
        The input to Stage 1 is the export from Runestone. A typical raw export contains many columns whose names and 
        meanings differ from one export format to the next, and several rows that are not student responses at all 
        (system events, retries, drafts).
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-1-input">
        <title>Stage 1 input: an excerpt of a raw export, before ingest.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="4%"/>
          <col width="18%" />
          <col width="22%" />
          <col width="56%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>...</p></cell>
            <cell><p>source_file</p></cell>
            <cell><p>question_tag</p></cell>
            <cell><p>typed_answer</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>...</p></cell>
            <cell><p>ula_s...rs.csv</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>There is a free variable so it is not possible to have a unique solution.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>...</p></cell>
            <cell><p>ula_s...rs.csv</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>(blank)</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>...</p></cell>
            <cell><p>ula_s...rs.csv</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                One and only one solution is not possible because there will be a free variable since there are more 
                variables than rows.
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>...</p></cell>
            <cell><p>ula_s...rs.csv</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>It depends what is in the augmented matrix.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Output: Clean RESPONSE Table</title>

      <p>
        The output is a single table where every row is one student response with standardized column names. The 
        columns that did not exist (or had inconsistent names) in the raw export are marked in italics in the header 
        row.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-1-output">
        <title>Stage 1 output: the same four responses after ingest.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="18%"/>
          <col width="16%" />
          <col width="24%" />
          <col width="40%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p><em>response_id</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>student_id</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p>question_id</p></cell>
            <cell><p>response_text</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-21</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>same as input</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-22</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>same as input</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-23</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>same as input</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-24</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1.4.8-pivots-q3</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>same as input</em></p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      <term>Primary new columns.</term> 
      <dl width="medium">
        <li>
          <title><c>response_id</c></title>
          <p>
            A stable, short identifier that lets every later stage refer back to this exact response without ambiguity.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>student_id</c></title>
          <p>
            The identifier already assigned to the student in the source data (e.g., the ID issued at enrollment in the 
            study). The tool preserves the identifier as given, so that the same student can be recognized across 
            responses, questions, and data files. This identifier also encodes textbook and instructor, which makes 
            later analyses by instructor or textbook section possible without adding separate columns.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Design considerations.</term> The main decision for this stage is how much context should travel with each 
      response. At minimum, the cleaned table includes the response text, the student identifier, and a question 
      identifier. The following additional fields are carried through because each one supports a later analysis or 
      audit the group is likely to want:
      <ul marker="square">
        <li>
          <term>Original row number</term> from the source export, so that any row in the cleaned table can be traced 
          back to its exact line in the raw file when a question comes up later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Source file path and hash</term>, recorded once per import, so that re-imports of the same file are 
          recognized as duplicates and skipped rather than producing parallel copies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Submission timestamp</term>, normalized to UTC, for any analysis that depends on when a response was 
          submitted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Course or section identifier</term>, when present in the source, so that responses can later be grouped 
          by instructor or section without re-deriving these from the student identifier.
        </li>
      </ul>
      A secondary consideration is whether to add a student table to the schema. This could be helpful if student-level 
      metadata (e.g., demographics, course section) is ever used in the analysis.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-stage-2">
    <title>Stage 2: Screener</title>

    <p>
      The <q>screener</q> decides whether a response contains enough interpretable reasoning to continue through the 
      pipeline. This stage does not assign a control structure. It only separates responses that appear codable from 
      responses that are blank, too brief, off-topic, or too ambiguous to analyze without discussion.
    </p>

    <p>
      A response is labeled <c>EXCLUDE</c> when it meets any of the following conditions:
      <ul marker="square">
        <li><term>Blank.</term> No response text was submitted.</li>
        <li>
          <term>Illegible.</term> The response cannot be read as a coherent attempt at the question (for example, 
          random characters or text unrelated to the prompt).
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Bare yes/no.</term> The response is only an answer (<q>yes</q>, <q>no</q>, <q>not possible</q>) with no 
          reasoning or justification.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Work-only with no verbal connector.</term> The response contains computation or symbols but no word 
          that links the work to a conclusion (<q>because</q>, <q>since</q>, <q>so</q>, <q>therefore</q>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Off-topic.</term> The response addresses a different question or concept than the prompt asks about.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Responses that do not meet any exclusion condition are labeled <c>INCLUDE</c>. Responses that are borderline are 
      labeled <c>FLAG</c> rather  than excluded, so a human reviewer makes the final call. The convention is 
      conservative on purpose: it is cheaper for the group to review a <c>FLAG</c> that should have been <c>INCLUDE</c> 
      than to discover later that a codable response was silently dropped.
    </p>

    <p>
      The screener does not decide whether a response is correct, whether it uses material from the textbook, or 
      whether it uses a control structure the codebook recognizes. Those are reviewer judgments and are addressed in 
      later stages.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Stage 2: Screener - Input &amp; Output</title>
      <sidebyside valign="middle" widths="25% 5% 15% 5% 22%" margins="14% 14%">
        <image xml:id="stage-2-in-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              RESPONSE
              RESPONSE {
                response_id *
                student_id *
                question_id *
                response_text *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-2-diagram">
          <mermaid>
            flowchart
              A(Screening)
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-2-out-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              RESPONSE
              RESPONSE {
                ⋮ *
                label *
                confidence *
                rationale *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Input: Clean RESPONSE Table</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-2-input">
        <title>Stage 2 input: cleaned responses from Stage 1.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="20%"/>
          <col width="18%" />
          <col width="62%" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>response_id</p></cell>
            <cell><p>student_id</p></cell>
            <cell><p>response_text</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-21</p></cell>
            <cell><p>There is a free variable so it is not possible to have a unique solution.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-22</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>(blank)</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-23</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                One and only one solution is not possible because there will be a free variable since there are more 
                variables than rows.
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>S-24</p></cell>
            <cell><p>It depends what is in the augmented matrix.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Output: Updated RESPONSE Table</title>

      <p>
        The output adds three columns: a screening status, a confidence number, and a one-sentence rationale that the 
        group can read alongside the response.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-2-output">
        <title>Stage 2 output: each response with a screening decision.</title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="16%"/>
          <col width="16%" />
          <col width="16%" />
          <col width="52%" halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>response_id</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>label</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>confidence</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>rationale</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0.96</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Connector ("so") and a named criterion ("free variable").</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
            <cell><p>EXCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0.99</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Blank response.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0.95</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Multiple connectors and multiple named criteria.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>FLAG</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0.78</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Potentially relevant, but criterion is meta-level ("depends on the matrix").</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      <term>Primary new columns.</term> 
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title><c>label</c></title>
          <p>
            Decides whether a response continues through the pipeline (currently <c>INCLUDE</c>, <c>EXCLUDE</c>, or 
            <c>FLAG</c>). The <c>FLAG</c> status indicates that a response is ambiguous enough to require human review.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>confidence</c></title>
          <p>
            A number between 0 and 1 that records how certain the tool is about the label that was produced. The 
            decision about what happens to a response is carried entirely by the <c>label</c> column. The 
            <c>confidence</c> column has two potential uses: it lets reviewers sort borderline cases to the top of the 
            queue when auditing the tool's output, and it provides the raw material for a future threshold-based 
            <c>FLAG</c> rule, should the group decide one is useful, without changing how the tool itself runs.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>rationale</c></title>
          <p>
            A short explanation of why the response received its label, which the group can read alongside the response 
            when calibrating the screener.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Design considerations.</term> The main decision for this stage is how conservative the screening should be. 
      A conservative screener keeps borderline responses in the pipeline or flags them for human review rather than 
      excluding them too early.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Possible directions for expansion.</term> This stage can grow along several independent paths, each of 
      which the same <c>(label, confidence, rationale)</c> output supports.
      <ul marker="square">
        <li>
          <term>Calibrate against a validated sample.</term> Run the screener against a small set of responses the 
          group has already labeled by hand, then adjust the criteria where the two disagree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Hybrid first/second pass.</term> Use a fast rule-based screener for the easy cases and call on a 
          language model only for the responses where the rules are uncertain.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Per-question screeners.</term> Tune a screener to the kinds of answers a particular question elicits, 
          since the patterns in responses to a pivot question differ from those in responses to, say, a span question.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>Mine the rationales.</term> The one-sentence rationales themselves are data. Clustering them surfaces 
          recurring reasons for exclusion, which can in turn refine the exclusion criteria above.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-stage-3">
    <title>Stage 3: Extractor</title>

    <p>
      The <q>extractor</q> converts a student response into a short sequence of reasoning steps. This stage does not 
      assign control-structure codes yet. It attempts to make explicit what mathematical claims or criteria the student 
      appears to use, in a small controlled vocabulary the tool can match.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Stage 3: Extractor - Input &amp; Output</title>
      <sidebyside valign="middle" widths="22% 5% 18% 5% 22%" margins="12% 12%">
        <image xml:id="stage-3-in-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              RESPONSE
              RESPONSE {
                response_id *
                label *
                response_text *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-3-diagram">
          <mermaid>
            flowchart
              A(Extraction)
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-3-out-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              CHAIN_STEP
              CHAIN_STEP {
                response_id *
                step_index *
                premise *
                conclusion *
                oov_flag *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Input: Filtered RESPONSE Table</title>

      <p>
        The input is a subtable of the <xref ref="tab-roadmap-stage-2-output" text="custom">RESPONSE</xref> where rows 
        (like R002) with <c>label == EXCLUDE</c> are filtered out until Stage 5.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-3-input">
        <title>Stage 3 input: included/flagged responses from Stage 2.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="20%"/>
          <col width="15%" />
          <col width="65%" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>response_id</p></cell>
            <cell><p>label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>response_text</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>There is a free variable so it is not possible to have a unique solution.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p> 
                One and only one solution is not possible because there will be a free variable since there are more 
                variables than rows.
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>FLAG</p></cell>
            <cell><p>It depends what is in the augmented matrix.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Output: CHAIN_STEP Table</title>

      <p>
        The output is one row per reasoning step, not one row per response. Each step is an abbreviated <em>(premise
        &#x2192; conclusion)</em> pair, expressed in the controlled vocabulary that the group can compare, count, and
        code. In Episode Model terms a step is the stored carrier of an episode <m>e_k = (C_k, I_k, J_k)</m>: the
        premise carries the criterion the student cites and the conclusion carries the judgment they draw from it.
        Steps whose premise or conclusion is not in the vocabulary are flagged with a <c>1</c> in the <c>oov_flag</c>
        column (<q>out of vocabulary</q>).
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-3-output">
        <title>Stage 3 output: each response as a reasoning chain.</title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="8%"/>
          <col width="5%" />
          <col width="30%" halign="left" />
          <col width="30%" halign="left" />
          <col width="17%" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>r..id</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>step</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>premise</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>conclusion</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>oov_flag</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1</p></cell>
            <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
            <cell><p>free_var_exists</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>2</p></cell>
            <cell><p>free_var_exists</p></cell>
            <cell><p>no_unique_solution</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1</p></cell>
            <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
            <cell><p>more_vars_than_rows</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>2</p></cell>
            <cell><p>more_vars_than_rows</p></cell>
            <cell><p>free_var_exists</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>free_var_exists</p></cell>
            <cell><p>no_unique_solution</p></cell>
            <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1</p></cell>
            <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
            <cell><p>solution_depends_on_b</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      <term>Primary new columns.</term> 
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title><c>premise</c></title>
          <p>
            The claim or criterion the student appears to use as a reason for something else &#8212; the episode's
            criterion <m>C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>conclusion</c></title>
          <p>
            The claim the student appears to draw as a consequence of the premise &#8212; the episode's
            judgment <m>J</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>oov_flag</c></title>
          <p>
            A flag indicating whether the student is reasoning about something the codebook does not currently have a 
            word for, flagging a potential new control structure that should be added to the codebook.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Design considerations.</term> The main decision for this stage is how much interpretation the tool should 
      be allowed to do. A cautious version only extracts reasoning that is clearly present in the student's words. A 
      more interpretive version fills in implicit links, but that increases the risk of attributing reasoning to the 
      student that they did not actually express. A second decision is the level of granularity for the splitting. For 
      example should the chain split the response:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <q>more variables than rows, therefore a free variable, therefore no unique solution</q>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      into three, two, or one step? Smaller steps are easier to match against the codebook, but larger steps may be 
      closer to how the student actually wrote.
    </p>

    <p>
      The working convention is to err on the side of smaller steps, since smaller steps make it easier to see when a 
      student is combining several criteria into a pattern that prior coding work has found to be both common and 
      consequential. The same convention can be revisited once enough chains have been produced to see whether finer 
      splitting changes downstream matching in practice.
    </p>

    <remark>
      <title>Possible directions for expansion</title>
      <p>
        The controlled vocabulary itself is the natural place to grow. Each OOV term that recurs is a candidate for 
        inclusion, with the group deciding whether it represents a control structure that should be added to the 
        codebook or content from a supplementary source that should be flagged as outside the textbook rather than 
        assigned a code. The same chain shape can also be used to record alternate readings of the same response side 
        by side (each as its own chain), so that disagreement among reviewers becomes a feature in the data rather 
        than a side note. A natural early experiment is to run the same responses through a literal extractor (which 
        encodes only what the student explicitly wrote) and a more interpretive extractor (which fills in implicit 
        links) and compare what each one keeps and loses.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-stage-4">
    <title>Stage 4: Matcher</title>

    <p>
      The <q>matcher</q> compares each reasoning step against the group's codebook. This stage does not make the final 
      coding decision. It records which control structures appear to fit the reasoning extracted in Stage 3, and 
      preserves every plausible match rather than deciding on a single winner.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Stage 4: Matcher - Input &amp; Output</title>
      <sidebyside valign="middle" widths="22% 5% 18% 5% 22%" margins="12% 12%">
        <image xml:id="stage-4-in-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              CHAIN_STEP
              CHAIN_STEP {
                response_id *
                step_index *
                premise *
                conclusion *
                oov_flag *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-4-diagram">
          <mermaid>
            flowchart
              A(Matching)
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-4-out-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              MATCH
              MATCH {
                response_id *
                step_index *
                code_id *
                rationale *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Input: CHAIN_STEP Table</title>
      <p>
        The input is the <xref ref="tab-roadmap-stage-3-output" text="custom">CHAIN_STEP</xref> table from Stage 3.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Output: MATCH Table</title>

      <p>
        The output is one row per <em>match</em> between a chain step and a codebook entry. A step can produce zero, 
        one, or several rows: the tool does not pick a winner, and it does not emit a row for steps that match nothing. 
        Each row has a short rationale so the group can see <em>why</em> a code was suggested. Steps that produce no 
        matches (R004's OOV) are bumped to Stage 5.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-4-output">
        <title>Stage 4 output: chains linked to control structures.</title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="10%" />
          <col width="10%" />
          <col width="14%" />
          <col width="66%" halign="left" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>r..id</p></cell>
            <cell><p>step</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>code_id</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>rationale</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>2</p></cell>
            <cell><p>CS2_3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A free variable is used to rule out a unique solution.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1</p></cell>
            <cell><p>CS6_3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Compares variable count to row count.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>1</p></cell>
            <cell><p>CS7_3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Alternate reading: compares unknowns to equations.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>CS2_3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A free variable is used to rule out a unique solution.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      <term>Primary new columns.</term> 
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title><c>code_id</c></title>
          <p>
            The ID of the codebook entry that matches the chain step.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>rationale</c></title>
          <p>
            A brief explanation of why the code was suggested. A reviewer can decide whether the tool's reading is 
            fair, generous, or stretched.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Design considerations.</term> The main decision for this stage is whether to return only the strongest 
      match or to preserve multiple plausible matches. For research coding, preserving multiple plausible matches is 
      safer because ambiguity is often exactly what the group needs to discuss; multiple matches also signal that a 
      student may be applying more than one way of thinking about the problem, which is itself data. The matcher 
      therefore reports every match the codebook patterns permit and leaves the disambiguation to human review.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Open naming question.</term> The column is currently named <c>code_id</c> for compatibility with the 
      database schema. A more framework-aligned name would be <c>control_structure_id</c>, which matches the language 
      used in the codebook and in Balacheff &amp; Gaudin (2010). The trade-off is that <c>code_id</c> is more generic 
      and would accommodate other kinds of codes (for instance, the criterion-correctness codes used in Deng et&#xA0;
      al. (2026)) without further renaming.
    </p>

  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-stage-5">
    <title>Stage 5: Disposer</title>

    <p>
      The <q>Disposer</q> decides what should happen next with each response. This stage organizes responses for human 
      review by identifying whether a response has a clear match, multiple possible matches, no match, or a prior 
      eligibility issue. It is the only stage that looks across all of the earlier stages at once.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Stage 5: Disposer - Input &amp; Output</title>
      <sidebyside valign="middle" widths="25% 5% 15% 5% 23%" margins="12% 12%">
        <image xml:id="stage-5-in-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              COMBINED_VIEW
              COMBINED_VIEW {
                response_id *
                label *
                code_ids *
                has_oov_step *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-5-diagram">
          <mermaid>
            flowchart
              A(Disposition)
          </mermaid>
        </image>
        <p><m>\longrightarrow</m></p>
        <image xml:id="stage-5-out-table">
          <mermaid>
            erDiagram
              DISPOSITION
              DISPOSITION {
                response_id *
                disposition *
                trigger_note *
              }
          </mermaid>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Input: COMBINED_VIEW Table</title>

      <p>
        The input is a combined view that gathers the <c>label</c> from Stage 2, the matched <c>code_id</c>s from Stage 
        4, and the OOV flags from Stage 3.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-5-input">
        <title>Stage 5 input: combined view across Stages 2, 3, and 4.</title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="15%" />
          <col width="50%" />
          <col width="25%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>r..id</p></cell>
            <cell><p>label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>code_id (aggregated)</p></cell>
            <cell><p>has_oov_step</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>CS2_3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>no</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
            <cell><p>EXCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
            <cell><p>no</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
            <cell><p>CS2_3, CS6_3, CS7_3</p></cell>
            <cell><p>no</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>FLAG</p></cell>
            <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
            <cell><p>yes</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Output: DISPOSITION Table</title>
      <p>
        The output is one row per response, with a disposition that tells the reviewer how this case should be handled 
        and a short reason that says why.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-stage-5-output">
        <title>Stage 5 output: each response ready for review.</title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="25%" />
          <col width="65%" halign="left" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>r..id</p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>disposition</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>note</em></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
            <cell><p>auto_accept</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Clear single control-structure match.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
            <cell><p>escalate</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Blank response excluded at Stage 2.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
            <cell><p>standard_review</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Multiple possible control structures.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
            <cell><p>terminology_flag</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Relevant wording, but reasoning uses a term outside the codebook.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      <term>Primary new columns.</term> 
      <dl width="medium">
        <li>
          <title><c>disposition</c></title>
          <p>
            This column turns the response table into a review queue by grouping responses into buckets that the group 
            can sort and filter. The current set of values are: <c>auto_accept</c>, <c>standard_review</c>, 
            <c>problem_type_flag</c>, <c>terminology_flag</c>, <c>no_match</c>, <c>escalate</c>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title><c>note</c></title>
          <p>
            A brief explanation of why the response received its disposition.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Design considerations.</term> One decision for this stage is how to sort the review queue. The 
      <c>disposition</c> values are deliberately coarse so that the group can rename or split them later without losing 
      data. A separate decision is whether <c>auto_accept</c> cases should genuinely skip review or whether every 
      response must pass at least one human eye.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Possible directions for expansion.</term> Dispositions can be paired with a small priority score so the 
      highest-value cases sort to the top. Reviewer assignment can be layered on top, with the same response visible to 
      two reviewers when the group wants double-coding. As the pipeline accumulates history, the <c>disposition</c> 
      column itself becomes the easiest way to measure how the codebook and the controlled vocabulary are evolving. For 
      example, a decreasing rate of <c>terminology_flag</c>s could indicate that the vocabulary is catching up to what 
      students actually write.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Open methodological question.</term> When one control structure appears as a sub-step of another within a 
      single reasoning chain (for example, a free-variable argument that is itself reached via a pivot-counting 
      argument), the group has not yet settled whether both should be coded, only the parent, or only the child. Stage 
      5 currently records every match Stage 4 produced, so that the data needed to apply any of these conventions later 
      is preserved. The convention itself is a coding decision rather than a pipeline decision and is tracked as an 
      open item for group resolution.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-end-to-end">
    <title>End-to-end Trace</title>

    <p>
      Putting the five stages together, each of the four sample responses arrives at a different disposition. The 
      single table below collapses everything the pipeline produced into one row per response, which is the view a 
      reviewer's dashboard would render.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-roadmap-end-to-end">
      <title>End-to-end trace: one row per response across five stages.</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="10%"/>
        <col width="12%" />
        <col width="20%" />
        <col width="36%"/>
        <col width="22%" />
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>r..id</p></cell>
          <cell><p>label</p></cell>
          <cell><p>chain steps</p></cell>
          <cell><p>code_ids</p></cell>
          <cell><p>disposition</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>R001</p></cell>
          <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
          <cell><p>2</p></cell>
          <cell><p>CS2_3</p></cell>
          <cell><p>auto_accept</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>R002</p></cell>
          <cell><p>EXCLUDE</p></cell>
          <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
          <cell><p>escalate</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>R003</p></cell>
          <cell><p>INCLUDE</p></cell>
          <cell><p>3</p></cell>
          <cell><p>CS2_3, CS6_3, CS7_3</p></cell>
          <cell><p>standard_review</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>R004</p></cell>
          <cell><p>FLAG</p></cell>
          <cell><p>1 (OOV)</p></cell>
          <cell><p>&#x2014;</p></cell>
          <cell><p>terminology_flag</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Every cell in every table is a single query away. Clicking any row in the eventual reviewer interface would 
      expand it to show the full chain, the full set of matches, and the rationale recorded at each stage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-overview">
    <title>Overview</title>
    <image xml:id="my-mermaid-diagram2">
      <description>
        <p>
          End-to-end flow of the pipeline: ingest, eligibility, reasoning extraction, codebook matching, disposition, 
          then human review.
        </p>
      </description>
      <mermaid>
        flowchart
            A[1: Ingestion] --&gt; B[2:Screening]
            B --&gt; C[3: Extraction]
            C --&gt; D[4: Matching]
            D --&gt; E[5: Disposition]
            E --&gt; F[Human review]

            B -. unclear / excluded .-&gt; F
            C -. extraction problem .-&gt; F
            D -. no match or multiple matches .-&gt; F
      </mermaid>
    </image>
  </section>
  
  <section xml:id="sec-roadmap-scope">
    <title>Scope of the Tool</title>

    <p>
      The tool now supports more than one <term>question type</term>. A service-agnostic core ingests and stores student
      response data, and one <term>service</term> per question type sits on top of it. This report describes the
      <term>short-answer</term> (control-structure) service; a separate drag-and-drop service is being built over the same
      core as the proof of concept that the design is expandable. Of the five stages below, the first&#x2014;<term>ingest</term>&#x2014;belongs
      to the shared core and serves every question type; the remaining four (screen, extract, match, dispose) are
      <em>specific to the short-answer service</em>. Another question type reuses that shared ingest and storage while
      supplying its own analysis stages.
    </p>

    <p>
      Within the short-answer service, the architecture is intended to be reusable across textbooks: the same five
      stages, the same database schema, and the same kinds of human-in-the-loop checkpoints apply regardless of which
      textbook is the source of the responses.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three components of the short-answer service, however, are inherently textbook-specific and would need to be redone
      for any new textbook the group wants to analyze:
      <ul marker="square">
        <li>
          <term>The codebook</term>, since the set of control structures that students might plausibly use depends on
          what the textbook teaches and how.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>The controlled vocabulary</term> used by Stage 3, since the canonical propositions ("free variable
          exists", "more variables than pivots", and so on) reflect the vocabulary the textbook uses to talk about the
          topic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <term>The validation data</term> for the LLM-integration work, since the human-coded set the service is
          calibrated against is a set of responses to questions from a specific textbook.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The short-answer service is built to handle multiple textbooks at a time, with the framework (control
      structures, criteria, cK¢ machinery) providing the cross-textbook continuity.
    </p>

  </section>

</chapter>